>

  <!-- § 01 the two versions -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>Two versions of every growth number</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Say India's GDP grows 9 per cent in a year. That sentence hides a question: 9 per cent more what? One answer is real output: 9 per cent more wheat, steel, software and haircuts. The other is prices. Or it could mean the economy produced the same amounts, only costlier. Usually it is some of both, and the two readings describe very different countries.</p>
        <p>So the statisticians publish growth twice.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Nominal <span class="sub">at current prices, rupees as paid</span></div>
          <p>GDP measured in the prices of the day. If output is unchanged but every price rises 5 per cent, nominal GDP rises 5 per cent. It counts more production and higher prices as the same thing: growth.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the size of the economy in the year's own rupees.</div>
        </aside>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Real <span class="sub">at constant prices, the price rise stripped out</span></div>
          <p>GDP measured in the fixed prices of a base year: 2022-23 for the current series, 2011-12 for the one before it. Prices are held still, so any increase must come from more output. When the news reports how fast the economy grew in a quarter, it is quoting the real figure.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> growth you could, in principle, eat.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">In an economy with rising prices, the nominal figure always outruns the real one. The gap between them is not an error. It is itself a number, and a revealing one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>The headline growth rate is the real one.</b> When the news reports growth, prices have already been stripped out. Nominal growth rarely leads a bulletin.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Nominal is not a lie.</b> Tax revenue, company turnover and your salary all arrive in current rupees. The question is which yardstick fits the question.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>Same year, two stories.</b> A 9 per cent nominal year with 8 per cent inflation is a near-stationary economy. With 2 per cent inflation it is a boom.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 the arithmetic -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The arithmetic, with toy numbers</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">A worked example, illustration, not data</span></div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Nominal GDP, toy economy</div><div class="v">+10%</div><div class="d">more rupees than the year before</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Prices, toy economy</div><div class="v">+4%</div><div class="d">the same basket, costlier</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Real GDP, toy economy</div><div class="v acc">≈ +6%</div><div class="d">what is left after prices</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The exact figure</div><div class="v">5.8%</div><div class="d">1.10 ÷ 1.04, minus one</div></div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:40px">Imagine a toy economy whose whole output is ₹100 of rice. The next year the bill is ₹110: nominal growth is 10 per cent. But suppose the price of rice rose 4 per cent over the same year. Then only part of the extra ₹10 is more rice. Divide the money growth by the price growth, 1.10 by 1.04, and the answer is 1.058: real growth of 5.8 per cent, which rounds to about 6. The rest of the headline was price, not output.</p>
    <p>The quick rule is the one to keep: real growth is roughly nominal growth minus inflation. Roughly, because the honest version divides rather than subtracts. At Indian magnitudes the two differ by a fraction of a point, so subtraction is the mental arithmetic and division is the official one.</p>
    <p>The same arithmetic runs your payslip. Suppose your salary rises 8 per cent in a year when inflation is 5 per cent. Your nominal gain is 8 per cent; your real gain is 1.08 divided by 1.05, or about 3 per cent. You are 3 per cent richer, not 8. The other 5 per cent was the price level climbing to meet your raise.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 the deflator -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>The deflator: the second inflation rate</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Nominal ÷ real</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Strip prices out of GDP often enough and a pattern emerges: the ratio of the nominal figure to the real one behaves like a price index for the whole economy. It has a name, and it is published with every GDP release.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Deflator <span class="sub">GDP's own price index, implied, not surveyed</span></div>
          <p>The ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP, read as a price level. Nobody goes out and collects it; it falls out of the national accounts once both versions of GDP are built. Because it covers everything produced, not only what households buy, it often differs from the CPI, sometimes by several points.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the CPI measures the inflation you shop with; the deflator measures the inflation the economy produces with.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">Two inflation rates, two jobs. The CPI asks what it costs a household to live. The deflator asks what happened to the price of everything made in the country: machines, exports, roads, government services. When commodity prices swing, the two part company for quarters at a time, and the gap between nominal and real growth tells you so.</p>
        <div class="break ledger" style="margin-top:48px">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>The question</th><th>Quote</th><th>Why</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="name">Did the economy actually produce more?<small>output over time</small></td>
                <td class="chg">Real</td>
                <td class="name">Prices are held still, so only output can move the number</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="name">How big is the tax base?<small>revenue, turnover</small></td>
                <td class="chg">Nominal</td>
                <td class="name">Taxes are collected in the rupees of the day</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="name">Is my pay keeping up?<small>raises vs prices</small></td>
                <td class="chg">Real</td>
                <td class="name">Divide the raise by inflation before spending it</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="name">How fast are company revenues growing?<small>top lines</small></td>
                <td class="chg">Nominal</td>
                <td class="name">Turnover arrives in the prices of the day</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="name">What is inflation doing inside GDP?<small>the second rate</small></td>
                <td class="chg">Deflator</td>
                <td class="name">It is the gap between the two, read as a price index</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iv</span>
          <b>Nobody surveys the deflator.</b> It is implied by the accounts, so it is revised whenever GDP is revised. Early readings of it are estimates too.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, v</span>
          <b>Two rates can disagree.</b> CPI and the deflator measure different baskets of prices. A gap between them is information, not a mistake.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Growth is growth.”</h3>
          <p>No. Ten per cent nominal growth with 9 per cent inflation is a near-stationary economy; with 2 per cent inflation it is a boom. The same headline, two different countries. Always ask which yardstick is in use before repeating the number.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Real growth is the government's trick to make numbers small.”</h3>
          <p>The correction runs both ways and cuts everyone equally. Strip out price rises and GDP shrinks to its honest size; strip them out of your raise and an 8 per cent increase becomes about 3. Nobody calls that manipulation when it applies to a payslip. The same division, applied to the whole economy, is not a trick either.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“My 10 per cent raise made me 10 per cent richer.”</h3>
          <p>Only if prices stood still. If inflation was 6 per cent, you gained about 4. The payslip is nominal; your life is real. Run the division first, then decide what the raise can actually buy.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. National Accounts Statistics: GDP at current prices (nominal) and at constant 2011–12 prices (real), with the implied GDP deflator published alongside. CPI combined on base 2012=100 is the household inflation rate referenced here.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>Nominal means current-price; real means constant-price. The quick rule, real is roughly nominal minus inflation, is an approximation; the national accounts divide. The worked examples use toy figures chosen for legible arithmetic, clearly framed as illustration. The one real print cited is the Q2 FY26 real growth of 8.23 per cent.</p>
    <p>Where a toy figure stands in for data, the page says so rather than borrowing authority it has not earned.</p>
  </div>
